I'm president of a company called  business laboratory and we build  computer simulations for businesses and  governments we use Mathematica in every  project that we do we build computer  simulation models and these are often  large systems designed to be replicas of  large organizations so we need the power  of something like Mathematica to do  those kinds of calculations to be  replicas of companies so that we can ask  a lot of what if strategy kinds of  questions of these models to help these  companies determine their best decisions  going forward we chose Mathematica  really because of three key things for  us speed power and flexibility  Mathematica does its calculations in a  enormously high performance way it can  work on very large very diverse data  sets including unstructured data sets  Mathematica can do just about whatever  we ask and perform any kind of function  we've used statistical functions we've  used the optimization functions we've  used a lot of the graphics primitives we  do a lot of our work in visualization  and mathematica gives us the flexibility  to put together very fine-grained  visualizations of the systems at hand  and these visualizations are critical  for allowing our complicated models to  communicate in non-mathematical ways to  our audience which are often company  executives our clients don't often know  what analysis is needed until they see  it so Mathematica ability to put  together very quickly these prototypes  of analyses that we could perform for  them allows them to see a bit of the end  games we're working on lots of different  projects right now one of the projects  that we're working on is with a chemical  company and the chemical company runs a  very complex set of processes to make  a nylon they came to us to ask us to  optimize the system optimize the  chemical supply chain for them we use  Mathematica to build that simulation  model and it enabled them to enhance  their profitability in a significant  sort of way I would say to anybody who's  doing serious work and analytics  Mathematica really is the the tool of  choice it's powerful it allows  integration with all kinds of data  sources and then you can build on that  analysis further by adding more complex  functions as necessary so it really is  the perfect environment for doing  serious work in technical computing
 

